1.1.text:
Article 5

The Regional Government is responsible for efforts to protect women and children from acts of violence in the form of:

a. physical violence;
b. sexual violence;
c. economic violence;
d. social violence;
e. psychological violence;
f. domestic neglect;
g. coercion or deprivation of liberty; and
h. threats of certain actions.

Article 22

The MSS as referred to in Article 21 has performance indicators including:

a. coverage of Women and Child Victims of Violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers within the integrated service unit;
b. coverage of Women and Child Victims of Violence who receive health services from trained health workers at Community Health Centers and Hospitals;
c. coverage of Social Rehabilitation services provided by trained Social Rehabilitation officers for Women and Child Victims of Violence within the integrated service unit;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Child Victims of Violence within the integrated service unit;
e. coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of Violence against Women and Children;
f. coverage of Women and Child Victims of Violence who receive legal assistance services;
g. coverage of Repatriation services for Women and Child Victims of Violence; and
h. coverage of social reintegration services for Women and Child Victims of Violence.

Article 8

(1) Regional Government conducts prevention of acts of violence against women and children by means of:

a. conducting socialization of laws and regulations; b. providing counseling/guidance;
c. providing education on the dangers of domestic violence; d. conducting seminars/workshops or the like;
e. establishing work networks in violence prevention efforts; f. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
g. establishing violence prevention systems, mapping of locations or areas prone to violence; and
h. increasing public awareness in behaving in accordance with religious norms.
(2) In addition to conducting prevention as referred to in paragraph (1), prevention of acts of violence against children is also carried out by means of increasing family capacity in child protection and parenting skills.
(3) In addition to Regional Government, prevention as referred to in paragraph (1) is obligated to be implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions;
d. health institutions;
e. social community institutions;
f. Pakraman Village; and jdih.bulelengkab.go.id
g. business sector and other institutions.

Article 2

Protection of Women and Children from Acts of Violence shall be implemented based on the principles of:

a. respect and fulfillment of the rights of victims of acts of violence; b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims of acts of violence; and e. legal certainty.

Article 4

Every Woman and Child Victim of Violence has the right:

a. to be respected for their dignity as a human being; jdih.bulelengkab.go.id
b. to obtain Protection from family, community, Regional Government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on a court protection order;
c. to physical, psychological and sexual health recovery according to the suffering experienced by victims of violence;
d. to special handling related to the confidentiality of Victims; e. to accompaniment by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. to spiritual guidance services; and
g. to settlement through mediation, if it constitutes the best settlement for the interests of the victim; and
h. to determine their own decision.

Article 14

(1) Regional Government conducts cooperation and coordination in order to ensure the implementation of protection for women and children from acts of violence;

(2) Cooperation and coordination as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by Regional Apparatus that administers governmental affairs in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.

Article 16

(1) In certain cases the Regional Government may cooperate with:

a. other regional governments;
b. the police;
c. advocates;
d. health workers; jdih.bulelengkab.go.id
e. social workers;
f. volunteer companions;
g. spiritual advisors;
h. other law enforcement officers, to assist victims in court proceedings;
i. the national commission on violence against women;
j. the Indonesian child protection commission or child protection institutions; and/or
k. certain parties desired for the benefit of victims.
(2) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of: a. socialization;
b. provision of legal assistance;
c. health services;
d. victim assistance;
e. counseling;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. spiritual guidance; and/or
h. repatriation and social reintegration.

Article 13

(1) Special protection of children is provided to:

a. Children in emergency situations;
b. Children in conflict with the law;
c. Children from minority and isolated groups;
d. Children who are economically and/or sexually exploited; e. Children who are victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic, and other addictive substance abuse;
f. Children who are victims of pornography;
g. Children with HIV/AIDS;
h. Children victims of abduction, sale, and/or trafficking; jdih.bulelengkab.go.id
i. Children victims of physical and/or psychological violence;
j. Children victims of sexual crimes;
k. Children victims of terrorist networks;
l. Children with disabilities;
m. Children victims of maltreatment and neglect;
n. Children with deviant social behavior; and
o. Children who are victims of stigmatization from labeling related to their parents' condition.
(2) Regional Government implements special protection of children as referred to in paragraph (1) in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations.

Article 21

MSS for Integrated Services for Women and Child Victims of Violence, includes services for:

a. handling complaints/reports of Victims of Violence against Women and Children;
b. health services for Women and Child Victims of Violence;
c. Social rehabilitation for Women and Child Victims of Violence;
d. law enforcement and legal assistance for Women and Child Victims of Violence; and
e. repatriation and social reintegration for Women and Child Victims of Violence. jdih.bulelengkab.go.id

Article 15

(1) Regional Government, within the framework of services for victims of acts of violence against women and children, establishes P2TP2A;

(2) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) functions as: a. information center for women and children;

b. service center for women and children victims of violence; and c. empowerment center for women and children.

(3) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) is obligated to: a. provide services as soon as possible and free of charge to witnesses and/or victims; jdih.bulelengkab.go.id

b. administer protection and fulfillment of the rights of witnesses and/or victims to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal aid;

c. cooperate with certain institutions in providing translators and accompanying volunteers needed for witnesses and/or victims;

d. network with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperate with witness and victim protection institutions, social protection houses or government-owned trauma centers, community or other institutions for health recovery of victims.

e. provide ease, comfort, and safety for witnesses and/or victims;

f. maintain confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims;

g. provide fulfillment of legal aid for witnesses and/or victims; and

h. in the event that integrated service delivery is carried out in a networked manner, be responsible for the entire service referral process required by victims.

(4) P2TP2A as referred to in paragraph (1) has the task of: a. providing integrated services to victims of violence; and b. providing protection to victims of acts of violence, reporters and witnesses.

(5) Provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (4) letter a is implemented in accordance with Standard Operating Procedures established by the Regional Government.

(6) Appointment of P2TP2A administrators as referred to in paragraph (1) is stipulated by Regent Decree.

Article 7

Protection of child victims of acts of violence is implemented by: a. formulating policies for prevention, Reduction of vulnerable risks, Handling of victims and data and information systems for children;

b. providing maintenance, care, and social rehabilitation for abandoned children, both within and outside institutions;

c. providing shelter, maintenance and care for abandoned children; and

d. providing special protection to children.

Article 11

(1) Regional Government implements integrated services for victims of acts of violence through activities:

a. complaint services;
b. health services;
c. counseling;
d. spiritual guidance;
e. social rehabilitation services;
f. legal aid and assistance services; and
g. repatriation and reintegration.
(2) Services as referred to in paragraph (1) must be supported by facilities in the form of:
a. special service rooms within police ranks;
b. expert and professional personnel;
c. service centers and safe houses; and/or
d. other facilities and infrastructure necessary for victim recovery. (3) Services as referred to in paragraph (1), including the preparation of visum et repertum, are carried out free of charge. jdih.bulelengkab.go.id
(4) Implementation of services for victims of acts of violence is carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government together with P2TP2A with other social institutions;
(5) P2TP2A and other social institutions as referred to in paragraph (4) may receive and send case referrals from other service units in a network;
(6) Integrated services for victims of acts of violence as referred to in paragraph (1), paragraph (2), paragraph (3) and paragraph (4) are implemented in accordance with Standard Operating Procedures;
(7) Provisions regarding Standard Operating Procedures as referred to in paragraph (6) are regulated by Regent Regulation.

